// nebula ui: the icons, every picture a tool of the toolbox and a button of
// the dockers and toolbars shows. Nebula draws them itself: each is a small
// function that builds a picture on a 24 x 24 grid out of strokes (two
// units wide, round ends and joins) and a few filled figures, with
// std.ui.vector; it is drawn once at the size and in the colour asked,
// kept, and from then on copied onto the canvas.
//
//     icons.draw(canvas, icons.TOOL_BRUSH, x, y, 24, theme.*.text)
//     const key = icons.name(icons.TOOL_BRUSH)        "tool-brush"
//     const icon = icons.named("tool-brush")          icons.TOOL_BRUSH (NONE: no such key)
//     icons.release()                                 when the program ends
//
// The colour is a canvas colour (premultiplied 0xAARRGGBB): a picture is
// that colour at the share of each pixel its figures cover, nothing else.
// The numbers of the icons are stable: a new one is added at the end of
// its table, never in between (settings keep the keys, not the numbers).
//
// Pictures stay sharp at every size because the grid is fitted to the
// pixels of the size asked: a stroke is a whole number of pixels wide
// (1 at 16, 2 at 20 and 24, 3 at 32, 4 at 48), the points of strokes lie
// on pixel middles or pixel corners as that width needs, and the edges of
// filled figures on pixel edges; all of it mirrored about the picture's
// middle, so what is symmetric on the grid stays so.
//
// The kept pictures are bounded (CACHE_PICTURES of them and CACHE_BYTES of
// pixels at most: the one drawn longest ago goes first). On a canvas of
// no pixels (a layout pass) nothing is drawn and nothing made. Nebula
// draws on the CPU; on a GPU canvas a picture goes as an image, and one
// that took the memory of another may show the other until the host
// forgets its images (not handled here: there is no window of Nebula's on
// the GPU).
const std = @import("std")
const math = @import("std.math")
const leaks = @import("std.leaks")
const png = @import("std.png")
const vector = @import("std.ui.vector")
const canvas_module = @import("std.ui.canvas")
const pixel = @import("../core/pixel.mlx")
const table = @import("../core/table.mlx")

const Allocator = std.allocator.Allocator
const Canvas = canvas_module.Canvas
const Shape = vector.Shape

